{"summary":"Source fidelity is strong. I built a ledger tracing every independent and dependent claim limitation, all 29 numerals in draft/numerals.md, and every structure, relationship, sequence and variant in the summary, detailed description and the four figure briefs back to an exact passage in input/disclosure.md (which input/conversation.md repeats verbatim). Everything traced: cylindrical housing with axial inlet/outlet, fixed metering sleeve, cylindrical dose chamber, radial concentrate port, carrier-liquid admission port, flush bypass opening downstream, the two-land/reduced-waist piston, the close-before-uncover isolation ordering, the one-way outlet valve, the index collar with helical cam groove and follower pin on the piston rod, one increment per full stroke, three axial stop faces / three detent recesses / spring-biased detent / transparent window showing one of three numerals, the flexible lip on the upstream land, the normally closed flush poppet, the annular rinse channel, the two radial rinse openings directed across the concentrate port and the outlet valve, the poppet closing before the waist reconnects, and the cartridge retained by a threaded end cap. The brief's preserved terminology is used consistently, one name per element, no means-plus-function language, abstract is 145 words. Two wording items from the previous review (claim 9 "the shown", claim 11 "removal") have been corrected in the current text. I found no internal contradiction between summary, description, claims and briefs, and each brief's numeral targets agree with numerals.md and with what the description says each figure shows.

Two scope limits on this review. First, figures/ contains only the four Markdown briefs — there are no rendered-*.png files and review/figure-audit-evidence.json does not exist, so no pixel, OCR, leader, hatch or endpoint verification was possible; nothing here is evidence that eventual sheets will be correct. One correction to the mechanical output: the drawing plan itself is not deficient. The four briefs already assign all 29 numerals (10–66) with explicit targets, so "redistribute the existing drawing plan" is not the remedy needed — the briefs simply have not been rendered. Second, prior_art/INDEX.md lists no references ("| (none) | | |") and the draft contains no [REF:] citations, so the citation check is vacuously clean; per input/brief.md no prior-art search was run, and the art available to this review is incomplete.

Three findings: one blocking formal defect (no drawing sheets exist), and two minor items — uncited factual assertions about existing devices in the background, and one added withdrawal-direction detail in the cartridge paragraph.","findings":[{"severity":"major","category":"formalities","title":"All four described figures exist only as Markdown briefs; no drawing sheet has been rendered","where":"figures/ (FIG-1.md, FIG-2.md, FIG-3.md, FIG-4.md); draft/07-drawings.md; review/figure-audit-evidence.json (absent)","detail":"draft/07-drawings.md describes four figures and draft/08-detailed-description.md attributes specific content to each of them, but figures/ contains only FIG-1.md, FIG-2.md, FIG-3.md and FIG-4.md — there is no rendered-*.png for any figure, and review/figure-audit-evidence.json does not exist. This is the sole cause of both mechanical failures: the 29 numerals are reported "absent from every drawing" only because no drawing file exists to read them from. The mechanical remedy as phrased is misleading and should not be followed: the drawing plan is complete, since the four briefs between them assign every numeral in draft/numerals.md (FIG. 1: 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 52, 64, 66; FIG. 2: 18, 20, 22, 24, 30, 32, 34, 38; FIG. 3: 22, 26, 28, 44, 56, 58, 60, 62; FIG. 4: 36, 40, 42, 44, 46, 48, 50, 54 — 29 distinct numerals, 10 through 66), each with an explicit single target, and no numeral labels two different parts across sheets. Because no pixels exist and no audit evidence file exists, I could perform no visual verification at all; my review of section 2 is limited to brief-versus-text agreement, which is clean.","evidence":"draft/07-drawings.md: "FIG. 1 is a longitudinal sectional view of an inline liquid metering valve." / figures/FIG-1.md: "Numerals on this sheet: 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 52, 64, 66." / directory listing of figures/: only FIG-1.md, FIG-2.md, FIG-3.md, FIG-4.md — no rendered-*.png present; review/ contains only previous-qa.md.","fix":"Render the four existing briefs as drawing sheets (rendered-FIG-1.png … rendered-FIG-4.png) exactly as written, and generate review/figure-audit-evidence.json against those images. Do not add, move or redistribute any numeral and do not delete any numeral definition or supporting text: the brief-to-numerals.md mapping is already complete and consistent, so rendering alone clears both mechanical failures."},{"severity":"minor","category":"citations","title":"Background asserts specific facts about existing dosing devices that no source in the workspace supports","where":"draft/05-background.md, paragraph 3 ("Two practical difficulties dominate…") and paragraph 5 ("The second difficulty is residue.")","detail":"prior_art/INDEX.md lists no references at all, and the draft carries no [REF:] citation, so nothing in the workspace supports statements of fact about third-party devices. input/disclosure.md and input/conversation.md say only that the valve "automatically rinses the surfaces that contacted the concentrate after each dose" and that the dose setting is "mechanically visible without electronics"; they say nothing about what existing devices do, how they fail, how they are cleaned, or what a rinse ordinarily requires. Two passages nonetheless assert concrete propositions about the art: what separately actuated shut-off devices depend on and how they fail, and what the "usual" remedy and "commonly" provided rinse are. Uncited background statements of this kind can be read as applicant admissions about the art. The equivalent passage in the dose-selection paragraph has already been recast into need-framed wording ("Adjustable dosing devices may be set by a screw or by an electronically stored value. There remains a need for…"), so the same treatment applied here restores consistency across the section. I mark this minor because it is problem framing rather than a statement about the invention, and because the need it frames is supported by the disclosure.","evidence":"draft/05-background.md ¶3: "Devices that rely on separately actuated shut-off elements to keep the two sides apart depend on those elements being commanded in the correct order, so a control fault, a lost signal, or a slow-acting element can open both sides at once." ¶5: "The usual remedy is to break the valve out of the line and clean it by hand, which takes the installation out of service and exposes the person doing the work to the concentrate. Where a rinse is provided at all, it is commonly a separate operation that has to be commanded after the dose, and it therefore depends on an operator or a controller performing an extra step at the right moment."","fix":"Recast both as conditional need-framing, matching the already-corrected dose-selection paragraph. ¶3: "Where the two sides are kept apart by separately actuated shut-off elements, the separation depends on those elements being commanded in the correct order." ¶5: "Where the valve must be broken out of the line and cleaned by hand, the installation is taken out of service; and where a rinse is a separate operation commanded after the dose, it depends on that operation being performed at the right moment." Leave the rest of both paragraphs and the closing need paragraph unchanged."},{"severity":"minor","category":"disclosure_fidelity","title":"Cartridge withdrawal is given a direction the disclosure does not state","where":"draft/08-detailed-description.md, paragraph beginning "The cartridge 64 contains the piston 28"","detail":"The disclosure states only that removing the end cap withdraws the cartridge without disturbing the housing connections; it does not say in what direction the cartridge comes out, nor at which end of the housing the threaded end cap sits. The description adds the phrase "along the axis of the dose chamber 20", which is a positional/relational assertion about the withdrawal. figures/FIG-1.md is careful to disclaim exactly this ('The axial end of the housing at which the end cap ring and the mouth receiving the removable unit are drawn is likewise a convention only'), so the description is currently more committal than its own figure brief. I mark this minor rather than critical because the direction is arguably entailed by the disclosed structure — a unit containing the metering sleeve and the axially sliding piston, inside a cylindrical housing, retained by a threaded end cap — and I am not certain it is an addition rather than a restatement; the fix is a deletion that costs nothing.","evidence":"draft/08-detailed-description.md: "Removing the threaded end cap 66 withdraws the cartridge 64 from the housing 12 along the axis of the dose chamber 20 without disturbing the connections at the inlet 14 and the outlet 16" — versus input/disclosure.md: "Removing the end cap withdraws the cartridge without disturbing the housing connections."","fix":"Delete the four words "along the axis of the dose chamber 20" so the sentence reads "Removing the threaded end cap 66 withdraws the cartridge 64 from the housing 12 without disturbing the connections at the inlet 14 and the outlet 16, so that the cartridge 64 is replaced as a unit while the housing 12 remains in the line." No claim needs amending: claim 11 and claim 20 already recite the withdrawal without a direction."}]}
